<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Applying combination symmetry and solving combination equations for n</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>(a)</b> Given that \({}_{{@pn@}}C_{{@pr@}} = {}_{{@pn@}}C_x\) and \(x \neq {@pr@}\), find \(x\).</p>
<p>\(x =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p><b>(b)</b> Given that \({}_nC_2 = {@pK@}\), find \(n\).</p>
<p>\(n =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part (a): Using combination symmetry to solve \({}_{{@pn@}}C_{{@pr@}} = {}_{{@pn@}}C_x\)</b><br>
Selecting \(r\) items from \(n\) items leaves \(n - r\) items behind, which gives the combination symmetry property:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle {}_nC_r = {}_nC_{n - r}\)</p>
<p>Since \({}_{{@pn@}}C_{{@pr@}} = {}_{{@pn@}}C_x\) and \(x \neq {@pr@}\), \(x\) must equal the complementary count \(n - r\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle x = {@pn@} - {@pr@} = {@ta1@}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ x = {@ta1@} }\)</p>

<p><b>Part (b): Solving \({}_nC_2 = {@pK@}\) for \(n\)</b><br>
Expand \({}_nC_2\) using \(\displaystyle {}_nC_r = \frac{n!}{r!(n - r)!}\) with \(r = 2\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle {}_nC_2 = \frac{n!}{2!\,(n - 2)!} = \frac{n(n - 1)(n - 2)!}{2 \times 1 \times (n - 2)!} = \frac{n(n - 1)}{2}\)</p>
<p>Set this expression equal to \({@pK@}\) and multiply both sides by \(2\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \frac{n(n - 1)}{2} = {@pK@} \implies n(n - 1) = 2 \times {@pK@} = {@twoK@}\)</p>
<p>We can find \(n\) either by testing consecutive positive integers or by solving the quadratic equation:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Consecutive integers:</b> Since \({@pm@} \times {@pm_minus_1@} = {@twoK@}\), the larger consecutive integer is \(n = {@pm@}\).</li>
  <li><b>Quadratic equation:</b> Expanding gives \(n^2 - n - {@twoK@} = 0\), which factorizes as \((n - {@pm@})(n + {@pm_minus_1@}) = 0\). Since \(n \ge 2\), reject \(n = -{@pm_minus_1@}\) to get \(n = {@pm@}\).</li>
</ul>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ n = {@ta2@} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
